<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St Magnus Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1137">1137</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlendsson">St Magnus</a> Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, was founded in 1137 by Earl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rognvald-kali-kolsson">Rögnvald Kali Kolsson</a> in honour of his uncle, the martyred Earl Magnus. Built in red and yellow sandstone in the Romanesque style, it is the most northerly cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the finest surviving building of the Norse period in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The relics of both Magnus and Rögnvald were found in its pillars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-magnus-cathedral.jpg" alt="St Magnus Cathedral" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevekeiretsu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Magnus_Cathedral,_Kirkwall,_viewed_from_the_Bishop%27s_Palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkneyinga-saga">Orkneyinga saga</a>, Earl Magnus, who ruled jointly with his cousin Håkon from 1105 to 1114, was killed on the island of Egilsay on 16 April 1116 or 1117, when Håkon's cook Lifolf struck him on the head with an axe. Miracles were reported at his grave at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birsay">Birsay</a>. When the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> granted Magnus's nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rognvald-kali-kolsson">Rögnvald Kali Kolsson</a> the earldom, Rögnvald's father Kol advised him to win over the islanders by promising a stone minster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a> dedicated to his uncle. Magnus was canonised in 1135, and work began in 1137 under Kol's direction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Romanesque work is attributed to English masons who may have worked on Durham Cathedral, and alternates red sandstone from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a> with yellow sandstone from Eday, sometimes in a chequerboard pattern. In the late 12th and early 13th centuries the building was extended east, and in the late 14th century the west front was joined to it, bringing in the pointed arches of the Gothic style. Rögnvald was killed in 1158 while work was under way. In 1919 a box of bones, including a skull with a wound like an axe blow, was found in a column.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The bishopric passed from the province of York to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a> in 1152 and stayed nominally under Trondheim until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a> became an archbishopric in 1472, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>'s annexation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1468. People accused of witchcraft between 1594 and 1708 were held and tried in the cathedral, some in its bottle dungeon, Marwick's Hole. In 1614 government forces meant to destroy it after rebels hid inside, until Bishop James Law intervened. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon IV of Norway</a> was buried there in 1263 until his body could be taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> it has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> parish church, owned by Orkney Islands Council.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St Magnus Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Earl Magnus, murdered around 1117, was venerated as a saint, and his nephew Rögnvald Kali Kolsson began <b>St Magnus Cathedral</b> in Kirkwall in 1137.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-orkney">Earldom of Orkney</a></li></ul></section>
